=====================================================================
lisptc — control flow: cond/if/when/unless, case, and/or, loops,
break/return, try/catch

Run me:
  pnpm --filter @repo/interpreter exec \
    node --experimental-transform-types src/lisp.ts ../../examples/control-flow.ptc
=====================================================================
(echo "=== 1. cond / if / when / unless ===")

(echo "hello")

cond: first non-nil test wins; a single-element clause returns the test
value itself.
(echo (cond ((< 3 1) 'a) ((< 1 3) 'b) (t 'c)))
=> b
(echo (cond (nil 1) (7)))
=> 7
(echo (cond))
=> nil
if/when/unless are all macros built on cond.
(echo (if (> 5 3) 'yes 'no))
=> yes
(echo (when (> 5 3) 'a 'b 'result))
=> result
(echo (unless (> 3 5) 'ran))
=> ran
(echo "=== 2. case ===")

CL-style case: evaluates key-expr once, then runs the first clause whose
keylist contains a value eql/equal to it. A bare non-list key is treated
as a one-element keylist.
(defun day-kind (day) (case day
    ((sat sun) 'weekend) ((mon tue wed thu fri) 'weekday) (t 'unknown)))
(echo (mapcar day-kind (list 'sat 'wed 'xmas)))
=> weekend weekday unknown
a single non-list key still matches directly.
(echo (case 'x (x 'matched) (t 'default)))
=> matched
the symbol `t` heading a clause is the default and always matches; wrap it
in a list — t ... — to match the literal value t instead.
(echo (case 99 ((t) 'literal-t) (t 'fell-through)))
=> fell-through
(echo (case t ((t) 'literal-t) (t 'fell-through)))
=> literal-t
no matching clause and no default -> nil.
(echo (case 'nope ((a b) 1)))
=> nil
(echo "=== 3. and / or short-circuiting ===")

and/or evaluate left to right and stop as soon as the result is known —
the unevaluated tail is never touched.
(echo (and 1 2 3))
=> 3
(echo (and 1 nil (/ 1 0)))
=> nil never reaches the division
(echo (or nil nil 5))
=> 5
(echo (or 1 (/ 1 0)))
=> 1 never reaches the division
(echo "=== 4. while, with break/return ===")

while loops until test is nil; break/return can end it early.
(setq i 0)
(setq acc nil)
(while (< i 5) (setq acc (cons i acc)) (setq i (+ i 1)))
(echo (nreverse acc))
=> 0 1 2 3 4
break exits the loop immediately; the while form evaluates to nil.
(setq n 0)
(echo (while t
    (setq n (+ n 1)) (if (= n 3) (break))))
=> nil
(echo n)
=> 3
return value exits the loop immediately with that value instead.
(setq n 0)
(echo (while t
    (setq n (+ n 1)) (if (= n 3) (return 'stopped-at-3))))
=> stopped-at-3
(echo "=== 5. dotimes / dolist, with a result form ===")

dotimes: name bound to 0..count-1; the optional third spec element is the
normal-completion result.
(setq total 0)
(echo (dotimes (i 5 total) (setq total (+ total i))))
=> 10
dolist: name bound to each element in turn.
(setq longest nil)
(dolist (w (list "a" "abc" "ab")) (if (or (null longest) (> (length w) (length longest))) (setq longest w)))
(echo longest)
=> abc
break skips the result form entirely the loop evaluates to nil;
return skips it too, but supplies its own value.
(echo (dotimes (i 10 'completed) (if (= i 3) (break))))
=> nil
(echo (dotimes (i 10 'completed) (if (= i 3) (return 'early-exit))))
=> early-exit
(echo (dolist (x '(1 2 3 4 5) 'completed) (if (= x 3) (return 'early-exit))))
=> early-exit
break/return only ever affects the nearest enclosing loop.
(setq outer-runs 0)
(setq inner-runs 0)
(dotimes (i 3) (setq outer-runs (+ outer-runs 1)) (dotimes (j 10) (setq inner-runs (+ inner-runs 1)) (if (= j 2) (break))))
(echo (list outer-runs inner-runs))
=> 3 9 -- 3 outer iterations, each inner loop broken after j=0,1,2
(echo "=== 6. try / catch / error ===")

try evaluates its body; if it raises, the first catch whose kind
matches runs with the handler variable bound to the error, a plist
(:kind k :message m :value v) .
(echo (try
    (car 5)
    (catch (e)
      (list 'recovered (get-in e :kind) (get-in e :message)))))
=> recovered :type-error car: list expected: 5
error takes a message, or a kind, a message and a value. A catch names
the kinds it handles; one without a kind handles every error.
(defun safe-div (a b) (try
    (if (= b 0) (error :divide-by-zero "b is zero" a) (/ a b))
    (catch :divide-by-zero (e)
      (list 'error (get-in e :value)))))
(echo (safe-div 10 2))
=> 5.0
(echo (safe-div 10 0))
=> error 10
finally runs however the try exits.
(setq closed nil)
(echo (try
    (car 5)
    (catch (e)
      'failed)
    (finally (setq closed t))))
(echo closed)
=> failed, then t
retry evaluates a form again when it raises, at most n more times.
(setq tries 0)
(echo (retry 3 (progn
      (setq tries (+ tries 1))
      (if (< tries 3) (error :flaky "not yet") tries))))
=> 3
attempt turns an error into a value, so a batch keeps every success.
(echo (mapcar (lambda (b) (attempt (safe-div 10 b))) '(2 0 5)))
=> (:ok 5.0) (:ok (error 10)) (:ok 2.0)
(echo (mapcar (lambda (b) (attempt (/ 10 (car b)))) '((2) 0 (5))))
=> (:ok 5.0) (:error (:kind :type-error ...)) (:ok 2.0)
with-ok chains results and stops at the first error.
(echo (with-ok ((a (attempt (car '(4))))
      (b (attempt (* a 2))))
    (+ a b)))
=> (:ok 12)
break/return are loop signals, not catchable errors — try does not
intercept them, so they still reach the enclosing loop.
(setq caught nil)
(dotimes (i 3 'loop-finished) (try
    (if (= i 1) (break))
    (catch (e)
      (setq caught t))))
(echo (list caught))
=> nil -- the catch clause never ran
(echo "=== 7. progn (sequencing) ===")

progn evaluates forms in order and returns the last one; useful wherever
the grammar expects a single form e.g. one cond clause's body.
(echo (progn (setq i 1) (setq i (+ i 1)) (setq i (* i 10)) i))
=> 20
(echo "=== done ===")
